// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  SL-DATA.JS — Scripture Locator verse data, books, achievements
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

const SL_VERSES=[
{diff:1,text:"The Lord is my shepherd; I shall not want.",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:23,verse:1,maxCh:150,maxV:6,short:"Psalm 23 opens with David's declaration of God as his caring shepherd.",context:"Psalm 23 was composed by King David using the tender metaphor of God as a faithful shepherd who provides everything his sheep need. It is one of the most beloved passages in all of Scripture, commonly read at funerals and in times of distress. The phrase 'I shall not want' expresses complete trust in God's sufficiency and provision."},
{diff:1,text:"For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting life.",testament:"New",book:"John",chapter:3,verse:16,maxCh:21,maxV:36,short:"Jesus describes God's love in giving his Son for the salvation of all who believe.",context:"John 3:16 is perhaps the most widely memorised verse in the New Testament, spoken by Jesus to Nicodemus during a nighttime conversation. It summarises the gospel in one sentence: God's love motivated the gift of his Son, and faith in him brings eternal life. This verse has been called 'the Bible in miniature' by theologians across the centuries."},
{diff:1,text:"In the beginning God created the heaven and the earth.",testament:"Old",book:"Genesis",chapter:1,verse:1,maxCh:50,maxV:31,short:"The opening verse of the Bible declares God as the sovereign Creator of all things.",context:"Genesis 1:1 is the foundational statement of the entire Bible, establishing God as the Creator before time and matter existed. It stands as a declaration against all theories of a self-creating universe and sets the stage for the full creation narrative. Jewish and Christian traditions alike regard this verse as the cornerstone of their understanding of God."},
{diff:1,text:"And we know that all things work together for good to them that love God, to them who are the called according to his purpose.",testament:"New",book:"Romans",chapter:8,verse:28,maxCh:16,maxV:39,short:"Paul assures believers that God works all circumstances together for their ultimate good.",context:"Romans 8:28 comes in the middle of Paul's great exposition of life in the Spirit, offering enormous comfort to believers facing suffering. It promises that God is actively weaving all circumstances — even painful ones — together for ultimate good. This promise is given specifically to those who love God and are called according to his purpose."},
{diff:1,text:"Trust in the Lord with all thine heart; and lean not unto thine own understanding.",testament:"Old",book:"Proverbs",chapter:3,verse:5,maxCh:31,maxV:35,short:"Solomon calls us to wholehearted trust in God rather than relying on our own reasoning.",context:"Proverbs 3:5 is part of Solomon's wisdom instructions to his son, urging wholehearted reliance on God over human intellect. The phrase 'lean not unto thine own understanding' warns against the pride of self-sufficiency, a recurring theme in Proverbs. The following verse (v. 6) promises that acknowledging God in all ways will lead to straight paths ahead."},
{diff:1,text:"But they that wait upon the Lord shall renew their strength; they shall mount up with wings as eagles.",testament:"Old",book:"Isaiah",chapter:40,verse:31,maxCh:66,maxV:31,short:"Isaiah promises supernatural renewal of strength for those who patiently wait on God.",context:"Isaiah 40:31 concludes a magnificent passage of comfort written to Israelites facing exile in Babylon. The prophet contrasts the limitless power of God with human frailty, promising that those who trust and wait on God will experience supernatural renewal. The eagle metaphor describes soaring above circumstances with God-given energy that does not grow weary."},
{diff:1,text:"I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me.",testament:"New",book:"Philippians",chapter:4,verse:13,maxCh:4,maxV:23,short:"Paul declares that Christ provides strength for contentment in every circumstance.",context:"Philippians 4:13 is one of Paul's most quoted declarations, written while he was imprisoned in Rome. Though often read as a promise of unlimited ability, in context Paul is speaking about contentment in all circumstances — whether abundance or need. The source of this remarkable contentment is Christ himself, who provides inner strength regardless of outward conditions."},
{diff:1,text:"For I know the thoughts that I think toward you, saith the Lord, thoughts of peace, and not of evil, to give you an expected end.",testament:"Old",book:"Jeremiah",chapter:29,verse:11,maxCh:52,maxV:32,short:"God declares his hopeful plans for his people during their Babylonian captivity.",context:"Jeremiah 29:11 was written in a letter to Jewish exiles in Babylon, assuring them that God had not abandoned them despite their captivity. The surrounding context calls them to settle in Babylon, pray for it, and trust God's 70-year timeline for their return. This verse is among the most quoted in inspirational contexts, though its full meaning is rooted in a specific historical promise."},
{diff:1,text:"Blessed are the poor in spirit: for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.",testament:"New",book:"Matthew",chapter:5,verse:3,maxCh:28,maxV:12,short:"Jesus opens the Sermon on the Mount with the first of the Beatitudes.",context:"Matthew 5:3 begins the Sermon on the Mount, Jesus' most extended teaching found in the New Testament. The 'poor in spirit' refers to those who recognise their spiritual bankruptcy before God — those who humbly acknowledge their need for him. Jesus declares that such humility, not pride or self-sufficiency, is the entry point into the kingdom of heaven."},
{diff:2,text:"Now faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen.",testament:"New",book:"Hebrews",chapter:11,verse:1,maxCh:13,maxV:40,short:"The author of Hebrews provides the Bible's definitive description of living faith.",context:"Hebrews 11:1 opens the great 'Hall of Faith' chapter, which catalogs Old Testament believers who acted on God's promises without seeing their fulfilment. Faith here is not wishful thinking but confident assurance based on God's trustworthy character. The chapter then demonstrates this living faith through figures such as Abraham, Moses, and Rahab."},
{diff:1,text:"Charity suffereth long, and is kind; charity envieth not; charity vaunteth not itself, is not puffed up.",testament:"New",book:"1 Corinthians",chapter:13,verse:4,maxCh:16,maxV:13,short:"Paul's love chapter describes the patient and selfless character of genuine love.",context:"1 Corinthians 13:4 is part of Paul's celebrated Love Chapter, written to a congregation in Corinth that was spiritually gifted but contentious. Paul argues that spiritual gifts without love are worthless, and then describes love's qualities in practical terms. Verses 4–7 form one of the most quoted descriptions of love in all of literature, widely used in wedding ceremonies worldwide."},
{diff:2,text:"God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble.",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:46,verse:1,maxCh:150,maxV:11,short:"The psalmist declares God as an immediate and powerful source of protection in trouble.",context:"Psalm 46 is a triumphant declaration of confidence in God even in the face of catastrophic upheaval. This verse sets the tone for the entire psalm, celebrating God as a fortress against every threat. Martin Luther drew inspiration from this psalm when composing his famous hymn 'A Mighty Fortress Is Our God.'"},
{diff:2,text:"For all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God.",testament:"New",book:"Romans",chapter:3,verse:23,maxCh:16,maxV:31,short:"Paul establishes the universal sinfulness of humanity as the foundation of the gospel.",context:"Romans 3:23 is a pivotal verse in Paul's systematic argument for salvation by grace alone. Coming after a detailed diagnosis of human sinfulness, Paul concludes that both Jews and Gentiles equally fall short of God's holy standard. The following verse (v. 24) pivots to the solution: justification as a free gift through Christ Jesus."},
{diff:1,text:"For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God.",testament:"New",book:"Ephesians",chapter:2,verse:8,maxCh:6,maxV:22,short:"Paul declares that salvation is entirely God's gift, received by faith and not earned by works.",context:"Ephesians 2:8 is one of the clearest statements of salvation by grace in the entire New Testament. Paul emphasises that salvation originates with God's grace and is received through faith — not achieved by human effort or merit. Verse 9 reinforces this by excluding all boasting, while verse 10 affirms we are created for good works."},
{diff:1,text:"Have not I commanded thee? Be strong and courageous; be not afraid, neither be thou dismayed: for the Lord thy God is with thee whithersoever thou goest.",testament:"Old",book:"Joshua",chapter:1,verse:9,maxCh:24,maxV:18,short:"God commands Joshua to be courageous as he prepares to lead Israel into the Promised Land.",context:"Joshua 1:9 was spoken directly by God to Joshua as he assumed leadership of Israel following Moses' death. The triple command — be strong, be courageous, do not be afraid — emphasises the weight of the call ahead. The basis for this courage is not Joshua's own ability, but the promise of God's continuous presence throughout the conquest."},
{diff:1,text:"Go ye therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost.",testament:"New",book:"Matthew",chapter:28,verse:19,maxCh:28,maxV:20,short:"Jesus gives his disciples the Great Commission to make disciples of all nations.",context:"Matthew 28:19 forms the heart of the Great Commission, the final command of Jesus before his ascension as recorded in Matthew's Gospel. Jesus grounds this worldwide mission in his own universal authority, and sends his disciples to make disciples — not just converts — of all nations. The Trinitarian baptismal formula here has been foundational to the Church throughout history."},
{diff:1,text:"And God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes; and there shall be no more death, neither sorrow, nor crying, neither shall there be any more pain.",testament:"New",book:"Revelation",chapter:21,verse:4,maxCh:22,maxV:27,short:"John's vision promises the complete and permanent removal of suffering in the new creation.",context:"Revelation 21:4 belongs to John's breathtaking vision of the new heaven and new earth, where God dwells fully with his people. This verse is one of the most comforting in all of Scripture, promising the complete elimination of grief, death, and pain. It offers eschatological hope to all who suffer, pointing to a future restoration far beyond anything this present age can offer."},
{diff:2,text:"All scripture is given by inspiration of God, and is profitable for doctrine, for reproof, for correction, for instruction in righteousness.",testament:"New",book:"2 Timothy",chapter:3,verse:16,maxCh:4,maxV:17,short:"Paul declares the divine origin and fourfold practical usefulness of all Scripture.",context:"2 Timothy 3:16 is the foundational text for the Christian doctrine of biblical inspiration. Paul writes to Timothy, his young protégé, reminding him that all Scripture is 'God-breathed' — originating from God himself. The verse then lists four practical purposes: teaching sound doctrine, exposing error, correcting behaviour, and training believers in righteousness."},
{diff:3,text:"He hath shewed thee, O man, what is good; and what doth the Lord require of thee, but to do justly, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with thy God?",testament:"Old",book:"Micah",chapter:6,verse:8,maxCh:7,maxV:16,short:"Micah distils God's ethical requirements for his people into three profound commands.",context:"Micah 6:8 comes as God's answer to the question of how to properly worship him. It beautifully summarises the ethical core of Old Testament faith: justice in dealings with others, a disposition of compassion and mercy, and a posture of humility before God. This verse has been widely influential in both Jewish and Christian social ethics and activism throughout history."},
{diff:2,text:"For unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord.",testament:"New",book:"Luke",chapter:2,verse:11,maxCh:24,maxV:20,short:"The angel announces the birth of Jesus the Saviour to the shepherds of Bethlehem.",context:"Luke 2:11 is the angel's proclamation to terrified shepherds near Bethlehem on the night of Jesus' birth. The announcement packs three royal titles into one sentence: Saviour, Christ (the anointed one), and Lord. Luke's Gospel uniquely emphasises this announcement to humble shepherds, reflecting his consistent theme of God's grace extended to the marginalised."},
{diff:2,text:"Thou shalt have no other gods before me.",testament:"Old",book:"Exodus",chapter:20,verse:3,maxCh:40,maxV:26,short:"The first of the Ten Commandments forbids placing any other god above the Lord.",context:"Exodus 20:3 is the first of the Ten Commandments given by God to Moses at Mount Sinai. It establishes the foundational principle of Israelite faith: absolute exclusive loyalty to the God who delivered them from Egypt. The phrase 'before me' carries a sense of 'in my very presence,' emphasising that God tolerates no rival in the worshipper's heart."},
{diff:3,text:"Who can find a virtuous woman? for her price is far above rubies.",testament:"Old",book:"Proverbs",chapter:31,verse:10,maxCh:31,maxV:31,short:"Proverbs 31 opens its portrait of the godly woman by declaring her priceless worth.",context:"Proverbs 31:10 begins the famous acrostic poem about the virtuous woman, where each verse starts with a successive letter of the Hebrew alphabet. The poem describes a woman of exceptional capability, character, and faith — praising her wisdom, business acumen, and generosity rather than mere appearance. In Judaism, this passage is traditionally recited by husbands to their wives on the Sabbath eve."},
{diff:3,text:"Then Peter said unto them, Repent, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of sins, and ye shall receive the gift of the Holy Ghost.",testament:"New",book:"Acts",chapter:2,verse:38,maxCh:28,maxV:47,short:"Peter calls the Pentecost crowd to repentance and baptism for the forgiveness of sins.",context:"Acts 2:38 is Peter's response to the Pentecost crowd who asked 'What shall we do?' after his convicting sermon about Jesus' death and resurrection. It outlines three elements: repentance, baptism in Jesus' name, and the promise of the Holy Spirit. This verse has been central to theological discussions about the order of salvation and baptism across many Christian traditions."},
{diff:3,text:"He that loveth not knoweth not God; for God is love.",testament:"New",book:"1 John",chapter:4,verse:8,maxCh:5,maxV:21,short:"John declares that an absence of love exposes an ignorance of God's very nature.",context:"1 John 4:8 is one of the most profound theological statements in Scripture, equating God's very nature with love. John is not saying merely that God loves, but that love defines who God is at the most fundamental level. This verse comes in a context where John challenges readers to love one another, arguing that such love is the evidence of truly knowing God."},
{diff:3,text:"The Lord bless thee, and keep thee: The Lord make his face shine upon thee, and be gracious unto thee.",testament:"Old",book:"Numbers",chapter:6,verse:24,maxCh:36,maxV:27,short:"The Aaronic blessing invokes God's protection and gracious presence over his people.",context:"Numbers 6:24-25 forms the first two lines of the Aaronic Blessing, the oldest recorded liturgical benediction in the Bible, commanded by God for Aaron and his sons to pronounce over Israel. The three-part structure — blessing and keeping, shining and grace, lifting and peace — reflects a crescendo of divine intimacy. It remains in common use in Jewish and Christian worship services today."},
{diff:2,text:"In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God.",testament:"New",book:"John",chapter:1,verse:1,maxCh:21,maxV:51,short:"John opens his Gospel declaring Christ's eternal divine existence as the Word.",context:"John 1:1 opens the fourth Gospel with one of the most profound Christological statements in Scripture, echoing the opening words of Genesis. By identifying Jesus as the eternal Word (Logos) who was both with God and was God, John establishes Christ's full divinity from the very first sentence. Verse 14 then reveals this eternal Word became flesh and dwelt among humanity."},
{diff:2,text:"Thy word is a lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path.",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:119,verse:105,maxCh:150,maxV:176,short:"The psalmist celebrates Scripture as divine guidance illuminating life's path.",context:"Psalm 119 is the longest chapter in the Bible and an extended meditation on the beauty and sufficiency of God's word. Verse 105 uses the vivid metaphor of a lamp — not a floodlight, but just enough light for the next step — to describe how Scripture guides believers through life's uncertainties. The ancient Hebrew lamp produced only a small flame, making the image one of intimate, step-by-step dependence on God."},
{diff:2,text:"And be not conformed to this world: but be ye transformed by the renewing of your mind.",testament:"New",book:"Romans",chapter:12,verse:2,maxCh:16,maxV:21,short:"Paul calls believers to transformation through renewed thinking rather than worldly conformity.",context:"Romans 12:2 opens Paul's practical section of the letter, following eleven chapters of deep theology. The contrast between 'conformed' and 'transformed' draws on two different Greek concepts — one passive, one active — to show that spiritual renewal is intentional. This transformation begins in the mind, and its goal is the ability to discern and live out God's perfect will."},
{diff:2,text:"But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, gentleness, goodness, faith.",testament:"New",book:"Galatians",chapter:5,verse:22,maxCh:6,maxV:26,short:"Paul lists the character traits produced by the Holy Spirit in the life of a believer.",context:"Galatians 5:22 comes in the context of Paul contrasting the works of the flesh with the fruit of the Spirit. The singular 'fruit' suggests a unified cluster rather than separate items, all naturally produced by a life yielded to the Spirit. Verses 22-23 together present nine qualities that stand in direct contrast to the fifteen destructive behaviours listed in verses 19-21."},
{diff:2,text:"My brethren, count it all joy when ye fall into divers temptations; knowing this, that the trying of your faith worketh patience.",testament:"New",book:"James",chapter:1,verse:2,maxCh:5,maxV:27,short:"James instructs believers to view trials as opportunities to develop patient endurance.",context:"James 1:2-3 opens one of the most practical letters in the New Testament with a counterintuitive command: to rejoice in trials. The word for 'temptations' here means external trials or testings, not moral temptation. James argues that such trials serve as a forge for faith, producing the quality of steadfast endurance that marks spiritual maturity."},
{diff:2,text:"Casting all your care upon him; for he careth for you.",testament:"New",book:"1 Peter",chapter:5,verse:7,maxCh:5,maxV:14,short:"Peter invites believers to release their anxieties to God, who is deeply concerned for them.",context:"1 Peter 5:7 comes in the context of Peter urging humility before God, and the casting of care is an act of humility — an admission that we cannot carry our burdens alone. The Greek word for 'care' describes anxious, divided concern, and 'casting' is a decisive, deliberate throw. The grounding for this action is the beautiful declaration that God himself cares personally and actively for his people."},
{diff:2,text:"The Lord is my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear? the Lord is the strength of my life; of whom shall I be afraid?",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:27,verse:1,maxCh:150,maxV:14,short:"David opens with a triple declaration of God as his light, salvation, and strength.",context:"Psalm 27:1 is one of the most confident declarations of faith in the entire Psalter, opening with three metaphors: God as light, salvation, and strength. The two rhetorical questions — 'Whom shall I fear?' and 'Of whom shall I be afraid?' — are not expressions of bravado but of grounded theological confidence. David wrote this likely during a period of serious military threat."},
{diff:2,text:"For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace.",testament:"Old",book:"Isaiah",chapter:9,verse:6,maxCh:66,maxV:21,short:"Isaiah prophesies the birth of the Messiah and his five royal titles.",context:"Isaiah 9:6 is one of the most celebrated Messianic prophecies in the Old Testament, spoken approximately 700 years before Christ's birth. The five titles given to the promised child — Wonderful Counsellor, Mighty God, Everlasting Father, Prince of Peace — each describe a dimension of his divine-human nature and eternal reign. Christians understand this verse as fulfilled in the person of Jesus Christ."},
{diff:1,text:"Jesus saith unto him, I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me.",testament:"New",book:"John",chapter:14,verse:6,maxCh:21,maxV:31,short:"Jesus makes his exclusive claim to be the only pathway to God the Father.",context:"John 14:6 is one of the seven 'I Am' statements of Jesus in John's Gospel, spoken to his disciples in the Upper Room the night before his crucifixion. Jesus does not say he points the way, teaches the truth, or offers a life — he claims to be all three in himself. The exclusivity of 'no man cometh unto the Father, but by me' has been central to Christian mission throughout history."},
{diff:2,text:"But ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me both in Jerusalem, and in all Judaea, and in Samaria, and unto the uttermost part of the earth.",testament:"New",book:"Acts",chapter:1,verse:8,maxCh:28,maxV:26,short:"Jesus promises the Holy Spirit's power and outlines the global scope of the disciples' witness.",context:"Acts 1:8 is often called the thesis statement of the book of Acts, mapping out the geographic expansion of the gospel: from Jerusalem to Judaea, to Samaria, and then the whole world. This was the last instruction Jesus gave before his ascension, linking the Holy Spirit's coming directly to the disciples' mission. Luke's narrative in Acts then unfolds exactly this pattern across its 28 chapters."},
{diff:2,text:"For I am not ashamed of the gospel of Christ: for it is the power of God unto salvation to every one that believeth.",testament:"New",book:"Romans",chapter:1,verse:16,maxCh:16,maxV:32,short:"Paul declares his bold confidence in the gospel as God's saving power for all who believe.",context:"Romans 1:16 is Paul's thesis statement for the entire letter to the Romans. The word 'ashamed' was a social category in the ancient world — the gospel was associated with a crucified criminal, which was culturally humiliating. Paul's bold counter-claim is that this message carries the very power of God for saving everyone who believes, Jew and Gentile alike."},
{diff:3,text:"For the love of money is the root of all evil: which while some coveted after, they have erred from the faith, and pierced themselves through with many sorrows.",testament:"New",book:"1 Timothy",chapter:6,verse:10,maxCh:6,maxV:21,short:"Paul warns that the love of money, not money itself, leads to spiritual ruin.",context:"1 Timothy 6:10 is one of the most misquoted verses in the Bible — Paul writes that the love of money (not money itself) is a root (not the only root) of all kinds of evil. Writing to Timothy in the wealthy city of Ephesus, Paul identifies greed as the inner disposition that has caused some to wander from faith and brought themselves great personal suffering."},
{diff:2,text:"He that dwelleth in the secret place of the most High shall abide under the shadow of the Almighty.",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:91,verse:1,maxCh:150,maxV:16,short:"The psalmist declares the divine protection available to those who dwell close to God.",context:"Psalm 91:1 opens one of Scripture's greatest psalms of divine protection, which some Jewish traditions associate with Moses. The 'secret place' suggests intimate fellowship with God — a place of closeness beyond the outer courts of casual relationship. The 'shadow of the Almighty' evokes a mother bird covering her young with her wings, a metaphor expanded in verse 4."},
{diff:2,text:"And whatsoever ye do, do it heartily, as to the Lord, and not unto men.",testament:"New",book:"Colossians",chapter:3,verse:23,maxCh:4,maxV:18,short:"Paul instructs believers to bring wholehearted effort to all work as service to God.",context:"Colossians 3:23 comes in a section where Paul gives household instructions to various groups including workers and servants. The word 'heartily' translates the Greek phrase 'from the soul,' meaning with one's entire inner being. Paul reframes all daily labour — however mundane — as worship and service directed toward God himself, not merely to human employers or overseers."},
{diff:3,text:"It is of the Lord's mercies that we are not consumed, because his compassions fail not. They are new every morning: great is thy faithfulness.",testament:"Old",book:"Lamentations",chapter:3,verse:22,maxCh:5,maxV:66,short:"Jeremiah finds hope in God's unfailing mercies and compassions renewed every morning.",context:"Lamentations 3:22-23 stand as an extraordinary declaration of hope in the middle of one of the darkest books in the Old Testament, written amid Jerusalem's destruction. Jeremiah has just recounted overwhelming suffering, yet pivots to affirm that God's mercies have not run out. The phrase 'great is thy faithfulness' became the title of one of Christianity's most beloved hymns."},
{diff:2,text:"And he said unto them, Go ye into all the world, and preach the gospel to every creature.",testament:"New",book:"Mark",chapter:16,verse:15,maxCh:16,maxV:20,short:"Jesus commands his disciples to take the gospel to every person in the entire world.",context:"Mark 16:15 contains Mark's version of the Great Commission, striking in its brevity and universality. The scope — 'all the world' and 'every creature' — is without limitation. Mark's Gospel, the shortest and most action-oriented of the four, fittingly ends with this urgent, boundary-breaking command to carry the good news everywhere."},
{diff:3,text:"If my people, which are called by my name, shall humble themselves, and pray, and seek my face, and turn from their wicked ways; then will I hear from heaven, and will forgive their sin, and will heal their land.",testament:"Old",book:"2 Chronicles",chapter:7,verse:14,maxCh:36,maxV:22,short:"God promises restoration to his people when they humble themselves, pray, and repent.",context:"2 Chronicles 7:14 was spoken by God to Solomon after the dedication of the temple, and has been widely applied to national repentance in Christian contexts. The four conditions — humility, prayer, seeking God, and repentance — are directed at God's own people, not the surrounding nations. The three-part divine response — hearing, forgiving, and healing — reflects God's restorative character toward those who return to him."},
{diff:3,text:"Hear, O Israel: The Lord our God is one Lord: And thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thine heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy might.",testament:"Old",book:"Deuteronomy",chapter:6,verse:4,maxCh:34,maxV:25,short:"Moses proclaims the foundational Jewish confession of faith in one God who demands total love.",context:"Deuteronomy 6:4-5 contains the Shema, the most important prayer in Judaism, recited daily by observant Jews for millennia. The declaration of God's oneness forms the theological foundation of both Jewish and Christian monotheism. When Jesus was asked which commandment was greatest, he quoted these very verses, calling love of God the first and greatest of all commands."},
{diff:2,text:"The thief cometh not, but for to steal, and to kill, and to destroy: I am come that they might have life, and that they might have it more abundantly.",testament:"New",book:"John",chapter:10,verse:10,maxCh:21,maxV:42,short:"Jesus contrasts the thief's destructive purpose with his own mission to give abundant life.",context:"John 10:10 comes in Jesus' extended teaching on himself as the Good Shepherd, addressed to religious leaders who had cast out a man Jesus had healed. Jesus contrasts two kinds of leadership: the thief who comes to exploit, and the shepherd who comes to give life. The 'abundant life' (Greek: perisson) suggests life that overflows and is qualitatively richer than mere existence."},
{diff:2,text:"That if thou shalt confess with thy mouth the Lord Jesus, and shalt believe in thine heart that God hath raised him from the dead, thou shalt be saved.",testament:"New",book:"Romans",chapter:10,verse:9,maxCh:16,maxV:21,short:"Paul states the twin conditions of salvation: heartfelt belief in the resurrection and open confession.",context:"Romans 10:9 presents one of the clearest gospel summaries in Paul's writings, with two interacting elements: inner belief and outward confession. The resurrection is specified as the particular article of faith because it vindicates Jesus' identity and atoning work. Paul draws here from Deuteronomy 30, arguing that salvation — like the law — is not distant or complex but near and accessible to all."},
{diff:2,text:"Delight thyself also in the Lord; and he shall give thee the desires of thine heart.",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:37,verse:4,maxCh:150,maxV:40,short:"David promises that those who find delight in God will receive their heart's deepest desires.",context:"Psalm 37:4 is one of the most quoted verses on prayer and desire, but its logic is often reversed. The key is in the first clause: delighting in God reshapes the desires of the heart so that they align with what God himself wants to give. The 'desires' are not selfish wishes but the transformed longings of someone whose heart is oriented toward God."},
{diff:2,text:"Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new.",testament:"New",book:"2 Corinthians",chapter:5,verse:17,maxCh:13,maxV:21,short:"Paul declares that union with Christ produces a complete new creation in the believer.",context:"2 Corinthians 5:17 is one of Paul's most celebrated declarations of the transformative power of salvation. The phrase 'new creature' echoes Isaiah's language of God's new creative act and places the believer within the larger narrative of cosmic renewal. The passing away of 'old things' refers not merely to habits but to the entire old order of life lived apart from God."},
{diff:2,text:"Submit yourselves therefore to God. Resist the devil, and he will flee from you.",testament:"New",book:"James",chapter:4,verse:7,maxCh:5,maxV:17,short:"James pairs surrender to God with active resistance of the devil as the path to spiritual victory.",context:"James 4:7 provides a two-part strategy for spiritual opposition that has become foundational in Christian theology. The first movement is surrender to God — without this, resistance alone has no power. The second is active resistance, grounded in the authority of the one to whom we've submitted. The promise that the devil 'will flee' is an assurance rooted in Christ's own victory over Satan."},
{diff:2,text:"Behold, I stand at the door, and knock: if any man hear my voice, and open the door, I will come in to him, and will sup with him, and he with me.",testament:"New",book:"Revelation",chapter:3,verse:20,maxCh:22,maxV:21,short:"Jesus invites a lukewarm church to open the door to intimate fellowship with him.",context:"Revelation 3:20 is part of Jesus' letter to the church at Laodicea, the most rebuked of the seven churches, known for being neither hot nor cold. The image of Jesus knocking at the door has become one of the most iconic in Christian art. The sharing of a meal together symbolises restored intimacy and covenant fellowship between Christ and his people."},
{diff:3,text:"But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed.",testament:"Old",book:"Isaiah",chapter:53,verse:5,maxCh:66,maxV:12,short:"Isaiah prophesies the substitutionary suffering of the Servant who bears humanity's sins.",context:"Isaiah 53:5 is part of the fourth Servant Song, written approximately 700 years before the crucifixion and regarded as the most detailed prophetic description of Christ's atoning death in the Old Testament. Each phrase — wounded, bruised, chastisement, stripes — corresponds to specific aspects of Jesus' suffering. The verse's logic is explicitly substitutionary: he suffered so that we might have peace and healing."},
{diff:2,text:"Blessed is the man that walketh not in the counsel of the ungodly, nor standeth in the way of sinners, nor sitteth in the seat of the scornful.",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:1,verse:1,maxCh:150,maxV:6,short:"Psalm 1 opens by portraying the man who avoids ungodly influence as truly flourishing.",context:"Psalm 1 serves as the gateway to the entire Psalter, introducing its central theme: the contrasting destinies of the righteous and the wicked. The word 'blessed' (Hebrew: ashre) conveys deep inner flourishing and wellbeing. The blessed man is first defined by what he avoids before verse 2 describes his positive delight in God's word."},
{diff:2,text:"O Lord our Lord, how excellent is thy name in all the earth! who hast set thy glory above the heavens.",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:8,verse:1,maxCh:150,maxV:9,short:"David marvels at the majestic name of God revealed across the entire creation.",context:"Psalm 8 is a hymn of praise that opens and closes with the same exclamation, forming a literary bracket around its meditation on human dignity and smallness. David contrasts God's transcendent glory — set above the heavens — with the remarkable honour God bestows on frail, mortal humanity. Verse 5 is quoted in Hebrews 2 as a prophecy of Christ's incarnation."},
{diff:2,text:"The heavens declare the glory of God; and the firmament sheweth his handywork.",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:19,verse:1,maxCh:150,maxV:14,short:"The psalmist declares that the sky itself is a continuous proclamation of God's glory.",context:"Psalm 19 is a celebration of two books of revelation: the heavens (general revelation through creation) and God's law (special revelation through Scripture). Verse 1 opens the first half, asserting that creation speaks constantly of God's greatness without words. The Apostle Paul draws on this truth in Romans 1:20 when arguing that all people have access to the knowledge of God through what he has made."},
{diff:2,text:"Bless the Lord, O my soul: and all that is within me, bless his holy name.",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:103,verse:1,maxCh:150,maxV:22,short:"David calls every part of his inner being to rise in praise of God's holy name.",context:"Psalm 103 is one of the most beloved praise psalms in the Psalter, entirely dedicated to celebrating God's benefits toward his people. The self-directed command — 'bless the Lord, O my soul' — reflects the psalmist stirring up his own will to worship rather than waiting for feeling. The psalm goes on to catalog God's forgiveness, healing, redemption, and steadfast love that endures across generations."},
{diff:2,text:"I will praise thee; for I am fearfully and wonderfully made: marvellous are thy works; and that my soul knoweth right well.",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:139,verse:14,maxCh:150,maxV:24,short:"David praises God for the intricate wonder of his own design as a human being.",context:"Psalm 139 is a profound meditation on God's omniscience, omnipresence, and creative power. Verse 14 comes in the section where David reflects on God's intimate involvement in his formation before birth. The phrase 'fearfully and wonderfully made' has become a foundational declaration in Christian discussions of human dignity and the sanctity of life."},
{diff:2,text:"Train up a child in the way he should go: and when he is old, he will not depart from it.",testament:"Old",book:"Proverbs",chapter:22,verse:6,maxCh:31,maxV:29,short:"Solomon teaches that intentional godly formation in childhood shapes a person's lifelong direction.",context:"Proverbs 22:6 is one of the most quoted verses in discussions of parenting and Christian education. The phrase 'in the way he should go' carries the idea of training a child according to their unique design and calling, not merely imposing adult preferences. While understood by many as a guaranteed promise, scholars note that Proverbs operates as wisdom literature — describing patterns rather than absolute guarantees."},
{diff:3,text:"Iron sharpeneth iron; so a man sharpeneth the countenance of his friend.",testament:"Old",book:"Proverbs",chapter:27,verse:17,maxCh:31,maxV:27,short:"Solomon illustrates the mutual growth that comes through honest, challenging friendship.",context:"Proverbs 27:17 uses the image of a metalworker sharpening a blade against another piece of iron to describe the unique power of genuine friendship. The friction required to sharpen iron is a metaphor for the honest challenge and accountability that true friends provide. This verse is foundational to the biblical vision of community as a place of mutual growth and sanctification."},
{diff:2,text:"Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee: because he trusteth in thee.",testament:"Old",book:"Isaiah",chapter:26,verse:3,maxCh:66,maxV:21,short:"Isaiah promises complete, unbroken peace to those whose minds remain fixed on God.",context:"Isaiah 26:3 comes from a song of praise celebrating God's faithfulness to his people in the day of redemption. The Hebrew word for 'perfect peace' is actually shalom shalom — peace doubled — indicating completeness and wholeness rather than mere absence of conflict. The condition for this peace is a mind that stays, or is anchored, on God — a posture of deliberate, sustained trust."},
{diff:2,text:"Fear thou not; for I am with thee: be not dismayed; for I am thy God: I will strengthen thee; yea, I will help thee; yea, I will uphold thee with the right hand of my righteousness.",testament:"Old",book:"Isaiah",chapter:41,verse:10,maxCh:66,maxV:29,short:"God personally assures his people of his presence, strength, help, and upholding hand.",context:"Isaiah 41:10 comes in a section where God addresses a fearful and exiled Israel with a cascade of reassurances. The verse contains five divine commitments: God's presence, his identity as their God, strengthening, help, and upholding. The threefold 'yea' intensifies each promise, and the image of God's right hand — a symbol of power — assures the reader that this is no passive comfort."},
{diff:2,text:"For my thoughts are not your thoughts, neither are your ways my ways, saith the Lord.",testament:"Old",book:"Isaiah",chapter:55,verse:8,maxCh:66,maxV:13,short:"God declares that his reasoning and purposes operate at an entirely different level than humanity's.",context:"Isaiah 55:8 comes in a remarkable chapter of invitation, urging all who thirst to come to God freely. The verse establishes why God's ways of mercy are so surprising — they exceed human reasoning and expectation. Verse 9 continues by measuring the gap as far as the heavens are above the earth, placing God's thoughts in a different category from human calculation."},
{diff:3,text:"A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you: and I will take away the stony heart out of your flesh, and I will give you an heart of flesh.",testament:"Old",book:"Ezekiel",chapter:36,verse:26,maxCh:48,maxV:38,short:"God promises to replace Israel's hard, unresponsive heart with a new, living heart.",context:"Ezekiel 36:26 is part of one of the most remarkable restoration promises in the Old Testament, anticipating what the New Testament calls the new birth. God promises not merely to command new behaviour but to perform heart surgery — replacing a stone-cold, unresponsive will with a living, tender one. This promise is understood in Christian theology as fulfilled through the regenerating work of the Holy Spirit at salvation."},
{diff:3,text:"The Lord thy God in the midst of thee is mighty; he will save, he will rejoice over thee with joy; he will rest in his love, he will joy over thee with singing.",testament:"Old",book:"Zephaniah",chapter:3,verse:17,maxCh:3,maxV:20,short:"The prophet reveals God rejoicing over his restored people with joyful song.",context:"Zephaniah 3:17 is one of the most tender verses in the Minor Prophets, reversing the expected roles: it is God who sings over his people, rather than the people singing to God. The verse follows a declaration of judgment but arrives in the context of ultimate restoration. The image of God quieting his people with his love and bursting into song over them portrays divine delight that has become a favourite for devotional reflection."},
{diff:3,text:"Behold, his soul which is lifted up is not upright in him: but the just shall live by his faith.",testament:"Old",book:"Habakkuk",chapter:2,verse:4,maxCh:3,maxV:20,short:"God declares that the proud will fall, but the righteous person will live through faithful trust.",context:"Habakkuk 2:4 is one of the most theologically significant verses in the entire Old Testament, quoted three times in the New Testament by Paul and once in Hebrews. Paul uses it in Romans 1:17 and Galatians 3:11 as his foundational text for the doctrine of justification by faith. The reformer Martin Luther's reading of this verse became the spark for the Protestant Reformation in the sixteenth century."},
{diff:3,text:"Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse, that there may be meat in mine house, and prove me now herewith, saith the Lord of hosts, if I will not open you the windows of heaven, and pour you out a blessing.",testament:"Old",book:"Malachi",chapter:3,verse:10,maxCh:4,maxV:15,short:"God challenges Israel to test him in faithful giving and promises to open heaven's floodgates.",context:"Malachi 3:10 is unique in Scripture as the one place where God explicitly invites his people to test him — specifically in the area of faithful giving. The command to bring tithes to the storehouse was addressed to a post-exilic community that had grown neglectful in worship. The promise of an overflowing blessing in response to faithfulness has made this verse central to Christian teaching on financial stewardship."},
{diff:3,text:"Let us hear the conclusion of the whole matter: Fear God, and keep his commandments: for this is the whole duty of man.",testament:"Old",book:"Ecclesiastes",chapter:12,verse:13,maxCh:12,maxV:14,short:"Solomon concludes his philosophical journey by reducing all of life's meaning to fearing God and keeping his commands.",context:"Ecclesiastes 12:13 is the grand conclusion of Solomon's extended meditation on the meaning and vanity of life under the sun. After exploring wealth, wisdom, pleasure, and work — and finding them all insufficient — Solomon arrives at this two-part conclusion: revere God and obey him. The simplicity of the conclusion is intentional — amid all of life's complexity, the summary of human duty is remarkably uncomplicated."},
{diff:2,text:"After this manner therefore pray ye: Our Father which art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.",testament:"New",book:"Matthew",chapter:6,verse:9,maxCh:28,maxV:34,short:"Jesus introduces the Lord's Prayer by establishing that prayer begins with honouring God's name.",context:"Matthew 6:9 opens the model prayer Jesus gave his disciples, known as the Lord's Prayer or the Our Father. The prayer begins not with human need but with God's identity and honour — 'hallowed be thy name' — setting the posture of all true prayer as God-centred rather than self-centred. This opening address 'Our Father' was itself revolutionary, inviting an intimacy with God that was unprecedented in formal Jewish prayer."},
{diff:1,text:"But seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you.",testament:"New",book:"Matthew",chapter:6,verse:33,maxCh:28,maxV:34,short:"Jesus commands his followers to prioritise God's kingdom above all material concerns.",context:"Matthew 6:33 comes at the end of Jesus' teaching on anxiety and materialism in the Sermon on the Mount. After pointing to birds and flowers as evidence of God's provision, Jesus gives the definitive command: prioritise the kingdom. The word 'first' is crucial — it does not promise abundance apart from seeking God, but assures that those who seek God above all else will find their genuine needs met."},
{diff:2,text:"Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you.",testament:"New",book:"Matthew",chapter:7,verse:7,maxCh:28,maxV:29,short:"Jesus promises a responsive God who rewards persistent prayer with provision.",context:"Matthew 7:7 is part of Jesus' teaching on prayer in the Sermon on the Mount, and its three imperatives — ask, seek, knock — form a progression of increasing intensity and persistence. The Greek present tense indicates continuous action: keep asking, keep seeking, keep knocking. The following verse makes clear that the promise is grounded in the character of God as a generous Father who delights to give good gifts to his children."},
{diff:1,text:"Come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest.",testament:"New",book:"Matthew",chapter:11,verse:28,maxCh:28,maxV:30,short:"Jesus issues a personal invitation to the weary, promising rest for those who come to him.",context:"Matthew 11:28 is one of the most tender personal invitations Jesus extends in the Gospels, spoken in the context of his gratitude to the Father for revealing truth to the humble. The 'rest' (Greek: anapausis) Jesus promises is not mere inactivity but a deep ceasing of striving — the rest of those who have found their burden carried by another. Verses 29-30 clarify that this rest is found in learning from Jesus as a gentle and humble teacher."},
{diff:2,text:"Jesus said unto him, Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind.",testament:"New",book:"Matthew",chapter:22,verse:37,maxCh:28,maxV:46,short:"Jesus names love for God with the whole self as the single greatest commandment.",context:"Matthew 22:37 is Jesus' answer to a Pharisee's question about which commandment is the greatest, quoting Deuteronomy 6:5. By naming love as the first commandment, Jesus establishes that the entire law flows from a relational core rather than external compliance. Verse 39 pairs this with love for neighbour, and verse 40 states that all the Law and the Prophets hang on these two commands."},
{diff:2,text:"For with God nothing shall be impossible.",testament:"New",book:"Luke",chapter:1,verse:37,maxCh:24,maxV:80,short:"The angel Gabriel assures Mary that no word or promise from God can be impossible.",context:"Luke 1:37 is the angel Gabriel's answer to Mary's question about how she, a virgin, could conceive. The Greek word translated 'impossible' is the negative of dunamis — the very word for power or miracle — meaning literally 'no word from God will be powerless.' Gabriel grounds the impossible nature of the virgin birth in the omnipotence of God himself. This verse has become a cornerstone in Christian declarations of faith over impossible circumstances."},
{diff:2,text:"And as ye would that men should do to you, do ye also to them likewise.",testament:"New",book:"Luke",chapter:6,verse:31,maxCh:24,maxV:49,short:"Jesus distils ethical conduct toward others into a single principle of reciprocal goodness.",context:"Luke 6:31 is Luke's version of the Golden Rule, spoken as part of Jesus' Sermon on the Plain. Unlike a negative formulation ('don't do to others what you wouldn't want done to you'), Jesus states it positively — actively do to others what you would want them to do. This positive framing calls for initiative and generosity rather than mere restraint, and has become one of the most universally recognised ethical principles in human history."},
{diff:2,text:"And ye shall know the truth, and the truth shall make you free.",testament:"New",book:"John",chapter:8,verse:32,maxCh:21,maxV:59,short:"Jesus promises that genuine knowledge of the truth produces true freedom.",context:"John 8:32 is spoken by Jesus to Jews who had believed in him, promising that continuing in his word leads to knowledge of truth and freedom. The freedom here is not political or philosophical autonomy, but liberation from the bondage of sin described in verse 34. This verse has been widely quoted in secular contexts as a motto for education and free inquiry, though in its original context the truth that liberates is specifically the person of Jesus himself."},
{diff:2,text:"Jesus said unto her, I am the resurrection, and the life: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live.",testament:"New",book:"John",chapter:11,verse:25,maxCh:21,maxV:57,short:"Jesus declares himself to be the resurrection and the life before raising Lazarus from the dead.",context:"John 11:25 is spoken to Martha just before Jesus raises her brother Lazarus, and constitutes the fifth of seven 'I Am' statements in John's Gospel. Jesus does not merely promise resurrection — he claims to be the source and embodiment of it. The verse is often read at Christian funeral services because it grounds the hope of bodily resurrection not in a doctrine but in a person."},
{diff:2,text:"A new commandment I give unto you, That ye love one another; as I have loved you, that ye also love one another.",testament:"New",book:"John",chapter:13,verse:34,maxCh:21,maxV:38,short:"Jesus establishes love for one another — modelled on his own love — as the defining mark of his disciples.",context:"John 13:34 is spoken in the Upper Room on the night of Jesus' betrayal, following Judas' departure. Jesus calls this commandment 'new' not because love for others was absent from the Old Testament, but because the standard is new: love as Jesus himself has loved — sacrificially, unreservedly, and unto death. Verse 35 adds that this mutual love is how the world will recognise his disciples."},
{diff:2,text:"Peace I leave with you, my peace I give unto you: not as the world giveth, give I unto you. Let not your heart be troubled, neither let it be afraid.",testament:"New",book:"John",chapter:14,verse:27,maxCh:21,maxV:31,short:"Jesus bequeaths his own quality of peace to his disciples — a peace the world cannot offer.",context:"John 14:27 comes in Jesus' farewell discourse, a gift to his disciples facing his imminent departure. The peace Jesus gives is qualitatively different from worldly peace, which is circumstantial and fragile. His peace subsists independently of outward circumstances, rooted in his presence and the coming of the Holy Spirit announced earlier in the same discourse. The verse is one of the most frequently cited for Christian comfort in times of anxiety."},
{diff:2,text:"I am the vine, ye are the branches: He that abideth in me, and I in him, the same bringeth forth much fruit: for without me ye can do nothing.",testament:"New",book:"John",chapter:15,verse:5,maxCh:21,maxV:27,short:"Jesus declares his disciples to be branches whose fruitfulness depends entirely on remaining in him.",context:"John 15:5 is the heart of Jesus' vine-and-branches teaching in the farewell discourse, and constitutes the sixth 'I Am' statement. The abiding relationship is mutually described — 'in me and I in him' — making union with Christ the source of all fruitfulness. The absolute claim 'without me ye can do nothing' is not pessimistic but clarifying: all genuine spiritual productivity has Christ as its source."},
{diff:2,text:"These things I have spoken unto you, that in me ye might have peace. In the world ye shall have tribulation: but be of good cheer; I have overcome the world.",testament:"New",book:"John",chapter:16,verse:33,maxCh:21,maxV:33,short:"Jesus honestly predicts tribulation for his followers while anchoring their courage in his own victory.",context:"John 16:33 closes the entire farewell discourse of Jesus with remarkable honesty and equally remarkable confidence. Jesus does not promise an easy path — tribulation is certain — but grounds his disciples' peace not in circumstances but in his own completed victory. The perfect tense 'I have overcome' indicates a decisive victory already accomplished, placing the outcome beyond doubt even as trials continue."},
{diff:2,text:"Neither is there salvation in any other: for there is none other name under heaven given among men, whereby we must be saved.",testament:"New",book:"Acts",chapter:4,verse:12,maxCh:28,maxV:37,short:"Peter declares Jesus to be the sole name and basis for human salvation.",context:"Acts 4:12 is Peter's bold declaration before the Sanhedrin after healing a lame man. The context makes the statement all the more remarkable — Peter is speaking to the very authorities who had condemned Jesus to death. The exclusivity of the claim — 'none other name' — has made this verse central to Christian evangelism and to theological debates about religious pluralism and the uniqueness of Christ."},
{diff:2,text:"But God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.",testament:"New",book:"Romans",chapter:5,verse:8,maxCh:16,maxV:21,short:"Paul highlights the extraordinary timing of Christ's death — given not when we deserved it, but when we didn't.",context:"Romans 5:8 is Paul's demonstration of the quality of God's love by pointing to its timing. Human love typically responds to worthiness — we love those who are loveable. But God demonstrated his love by sending Christ to die for us while we were still his enemies (v. 10), not after we improved. This verse is foundational to the doctrine of grace — God's love is not a response to human merit but a gift given apart from it."},
{diff:1,text:"For the wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.",testament:"New",book:"Romans",chapter:6,verse:23,maxCh:16,maxV:23,short:"Paul contrasts sin's earned consequence with God's freely given gift of eternal life.",context:"Romans 6:23 is one of the most concise gospel summaries in all of Paul's writings. The structure is a deliberate contrast: sin pays a wage — death, which is what is earned — while God gives a gift — eternal life, which is freely given and unearned. The word 'gift' (Greek: charisma) is related to grace (charis), emphasising that eternal life cannot be earned but only received. This verse is widely used as the third step in the 'Romans Road' evangelism outline."},
{diff:2,text:"There is therefore now no condemnation to them which are in Christ Jesus, who walk not after the flesh, but after the Spirit.",testament:"New",book:"Romans",chapter:8,verse:1,maxCh:16,maxV:39,short:"Paul declares the complete absence of condemnation for those who are united with Christ Jesus.",context:"Romans 8:1 opens what many consider the greatest chapter in the New Testament, transitioning from the struggle of chapter 7 to the triumph of life in the Spirit. The word 'therefore' connects it to all of Paul's previous argument about justification by faith. 'No condemnation' is absolute — there is no partial verdict, no lingering guilt — for those who are in Christ, because Christ bore the full penalty in their place."},
{diff:2,text:"I beseech you therefore, brethren, by the mercies of God, that ye present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable unto God, which is your reasonable service.",testament:"New",book:"Romans",chapter:12,verse:1,maxCh:16,maxV:21,short:"Paul calls believers to offer their whole selves to God as an act of living, reasonable worship.",context:"Romans 12:1 is the pivotal turning point of the entire letter, where Paul moves from doctrine to practice. The word 'therefore' connects the appeal to the eleven chapters of theological argument that precede it. The image of a 'living sacrifice' is paradoxical — sacrifices were killed — but the Christian life involves continual, daily self-offering. Paul calls this 'reasonable service' (Greek: logiken latreian) — the fitting, rational response to all that God has done."},
{diff:2,text:"There hath no temptation taken you but such as is common to man: but God is faithful, who will not suffer you to be tempted above that ye are able; but will with the temptation also make a way to escape.",testament:"New",book:"1 Corinthians",chapter:10,verse:13,maxCh:16,maxV:33,short:"Paul assures believers that every temptation they face is matched by God's faithfulness and a provided escape route.",context:"1 Corinthians 10:13 comes in Paul's warning to the Corinthians against overconfidence, using Israel's wilderness failures as a cautionary example. The verse is both honest — temptation is real and common — and reassuring: God does not allow temptation to exceed what his grace can sustain. The 'way of escape' is not necessarily removal of the temptation but the provision of endurance and an exit path for those who look for it."},
{diff:3,text:"O death, where is thy sting? O grave, where is thy victory?",testament:"New",book:"1 Corinthians",chapter:15,verse:55,maxCh:16,maxV:58,short:"Paul taunts death and the grave with their own defeat, won through Christ's resurrection.",context:"1 Corinthians 15:55 comes near the climax of Paul's extended defence of bodily resurrection, the longest chapter in his letters. The verse is a rhetorical taunt drawn from Hosea 13:14, turned into a victory cry. Death, which had wielded its sting against humanity since the fall, has been disarmed by Christ's resurrection. Paul is not denying death's reality but proclaiming that its power over believers has been broken decisively and permanently."},
{diff:2,text:"And he said unto me, My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness. Most gladly therefore will I rather glory in my infirmities, that the power of Christ may rest upon me.",testament:"New",book:"2 Corinthians",chapter:12,verse:9,maxCh:13,maxV:21,short:"God tells Paul that his grace is enough — and that divine power is displayed most fully in human weakness.",context:"2 Corinthians 12:9 is God's spoken reply to Paul's three-fold prayer for the removal of a 'thorn in the flesh.' Rather than removing the affliction, God reframes it: weakness becomes the very condition in which divine power is most clearly displayed. This paradox — strength perfected in weakness — became one of Paul's defining theological insights, transforming how he understood suffering and dependence on God."},
{diff:2,text:"I am crucified with Christ: nevertheless I live; yet not I, but Christ liveth in me: and the life which I now live in the flesh I live by the faith of the Son of God, who loved me, and gave himself for me.",testament:"New",book:"Galatians",chapter:2,verse:20,maxCh:6,maxV:21,short:"Paul describes his identity as radically redefined by union with Christ — death and life simultaneously.",context:"Galatians 2:20 is one of Paul's most personal theological statements, written in the heat of his argument against those requiring circumcision for salvation. The paradox — crucified yet alive, I yet not I — captures the Christian experience of dying to self-reliance while receiving the life of Christ. The personal 'who loved me and gave himself for me' grounds the universal work of Christ in an intensely individual love."},
{diff:2,text:"Be not deceived; God is not mocked: for whatsoever a man soweth, that shall he also reap.",testament:"New",book:"Galatians",chapter:6,verse:7,maxCh:6,maxV:18,short:"Paul warns that the law of sowing and reaping operates without exception — even in the spiritual life.",context:"Galatians 6:7 comes in Paul's closing exhortations about supporting those who teach and walking in the Spirit. The agricultural metaphor is universal and intuitive: no farmer expects wheat from corn seed. Paul applies this principle spiritually — those who invest in the flesh will harvest corruption; those who invest in the Spirit will harvest eternal life (v. 8). 'God is not mocked' asserts that this law cannot be circumvented or outwitted."},
{diff:2,text:"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.",testament:"New",book:"Ephesians",chapter:6,verse:11,maxCh:6,maxV:24,short:"Paul commands believers to equip themselves with God's full spiritual armour to resist the devil's strategies.",context:"Ephesians 6:11 opens one of the most celebrated passages in the New Testament — the description of the armour of God. Paul uses the image of a Roman soldier's equipment to describe spiritual resources available to believers. The word 'wiles' (Greek: methodeia) suggests the devil operates through strategy and cunning rather than brute force, making preparation and awareness essential. The passage concludes with an appeal to prayer as the atmosphere in which the armour functions."},
{diff:3,text:"Rejoice evermore. Pray without ceasing. In every thing give thanks: for this is the will of God in Christ Jesus concerning you.",testament:"New",book:"1 Thessalonians",chapter:5,verse:16,maxCh:5,maxV:28,short:"Paul gives three tightly compressed commands that together describe the posture of the Spirit-filled life.",context:"1 Thessalonians 5:16-18 form the three shortest commands in Paul's letters, sometimes called his trilogy of spiritual life. 'Rejoice evermore' does not demand manufactured happiness but a settled orientation of gratitude. 'Pray without ceasing' suggests a continuous atmosphere of communication with God rather than uninterrupted words. 'Give thanks in everything' is distinguished from giving thanks for everything — it is a discipline of the will in all circumstances."},
{diff:2,text:"For the word of God is quick, and powerful, and sharper than any twoedged sword, piercing even to the dividing asunder of soul and spirit, and of the joints and marrow, and is a discerner of the thoughts and intents of the heart.",testament:"New",book:"Hebrews",chapter:4,verse:12,maxCh:13,maxV:16,short:"The author declares Scripture to be a living, penetrating instrument that discerns the deepest human motives.",context:"Hebrews 4:12 comes in the context of a warning not to harden the heart as Israel did in the wilderness. The word of God is described with four attributes: living (quick), powerful (active), sharp like a two-edged sword, and penetrating to divide what no surgeon's blade can. The final description — discerner of thoughts and intents — establishes that Scripture does not merely inform the mind but exposes the heart."},
{diff:3,text:"For as the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is dead also.",testament:"New",book:"James",chapter:2,verse:26,maxCh:5,maxV:26,short:"James concludes his argument that genuine faith necessarily produces visible action and works.",context:"James 2:26 is the capstone of James' famous argument about faith and works, which has sometimes been seen as contradicting Paul's teaching on faith alone. The resolution is that James and Paul are addressing different errors: Paul opposes works-righteousness (trying to earn salvation), while James opposes dead orthodoxy (claiming faith without any evidence in behaviour). The analogy to a body without breath is stark — lifeless faith is not merely weak faith but no faith at all."},
{diff:2,text:"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.",testament:"New",book:"1 John",chapter:1,verse:9,maxCh:5,maxV:10,short:"John promises that honest confession of sin is met by God's faithful and just forgiveness and cleansing.",context:"1 John 1:9 is written to believers who may be tempted to deny or minimise their sin, and it provides the remedy. Confession (Greek: homologein) means to agree with God about sin — not merely to admit it but to see it as he sees it. The basis for forgiveness is God's character: he is both faithful (to his covenant) and just (because Christ's atoning death satisfied justice), not merely generous."},
{diff:3,text:"Now unto him that is able to keep you from falling, and to present you faultless before the presence of his glory with exceeding joy.",testament:"New",book:"Jude",chapter:1,verse:24,maxCh:1,maxV:25,short:"Jude's doxology celebrates God's power to preserve believers and present them blameless before his glory.",context:"Jude 1:24 forms the opening of one of the most celebrated doxologies in the New Testament, concluding one of the shortest and most urgent letters of the Bible. After warning extensively about false teachers threatening the church, Jude ends not with despair but with doxology — the power of God to keep and present his people. 'Faultless' (Greek: amomos) is the word used for a sacrificial animal without blemish, suggesting perfected purity."},
{diff:3,text:"I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the ending, saith the Lord, which is, and which was, and which is to come, the Almighty.",testament:"New",book:"Revelation",chapter:1,verse:8,maxCh:22,maxV:20,short:"The Lord God declares his eternal, absolute sovereignty using the first and last letters of the Greek alphabet.",context:"Revelation 1:8 is the first direct speech of God in the book of Revelation, establishing his eternal nature before any of the visions are given. Alpha and Omega — the first and last letters of the Greek alphabet — declare that God encompasses all of history from beginning to end. The threefold description 'which is, was, and is to come' mirrors the covenant name Yahweh (I AM) and roots the entire apocalyptic vision in the character of the unchanging God."},
{diff:3,text:"O taste and see that the Lord is good: blessed is the man that trusteth in him.",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:34,verse:8,maxCh:150,maxV:22,short:"The psalmist invites personal experiential encounter with God's goodness rather than merely intellectual agreement.",context:"Psalm 34 is an acrostic psalm of David written when he feigned madness before Abimelech. Verse 8 uses the sensory metaphor of tasting to describe the personal, experiential nature of knowing God's goodness — not merely believing it theoretically. The invitation 'taste and see' suggests that divine goodness is not first an argument to be won but an experience to be entered. Peter quotes this verse in 1 Peter 2:3 in the context of spiritual growth."},
{diff:2,text:"Create in me a clean heart, O God; and renew a right spirit within me.",testament:"Old",book:"Psalms",chapter:51,verse:10,maxCh:150,maxV:19,short:"David cries out for inner transformation after his sin, asking God to create — not merely clean — a new heart.",context:"Psalm 51 is David's great psalm of repentance, written after the prophet Nathan confronted him about his adultery with Bathsheba and murder of Uriah. Verse 10 is the turning point from confession to petition: David does not ask merely for forgiveness but for new creation — the word 'create' (Hebrew: bara) is used only of divine action in the Old Testament. The prayer for a 'right spirit' indicates that David understands his sin as rooted in a disordered inner life, not merely outward failure."},
{diff:3,text:"But ye are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people; that ye should shew forth the praises of him who hath called you out of darkness into his marvellous light.",testament:"New",book:"1 Peter",chapter:2,verse:9,maxCh:5,maxV:25,short:"Peter applies Israel's covenant titles to the New Testament church, declaring their identity and calling.",context:"1 Peter 2:9 draws four identity titles from the Old Testament — chosen generation, royal priesthood, holy nation, peculiar people — originally applied to Israel in Exodus 19 and Deuteronomy — and applies them to the believing community of Jesus. The purpose clause is significant: this remarkable identity is not for privilege but for proclamation, declaring the praises of the God who called them from darkness to light."},
{diff:3,text:"Come now, and let us reason together, saith the Lord: though your sins be as scarlet, they shall be as white as snow; though they be red like crimson, they shall be as wool.",testament:"Old",book:"Isaiah",chapter:1,verse:18,maxCh:66,maxV:31,short:"God invites sinful Israel into honest dialogue, promising complete transformation of their moral stain.",context:"Isaiah 1:18 comes after God's devastating indictment of Israel's corruption and empty religious practice. Rather than declaring immediate judgment, God issues a stunning invitation to rational dialogue — 'come, let us reason together.' The colour metaphor of scarlet and crimson turning to snow-white and wool became one of the most vivid pictures of divine forgiveness in the Old Testament. The transformation described is not gradual improvement but complete reversal."},
{diff:3,text:"For whatsoever is born of God overcometh the world: and this is the victory that overcometh the world, even our faith.",testament:"New",book:"1 John",chapter:5,verse:4,maxCh:5,maxV:21,short:"John declares that everyone born of God carries within them the victory that overcomes the world.",context:"1 John 5:4 comes in a passage where John asserts that loving God and keeping his commands is not burdensome. The claim that 'whatsoever is born of God overcometh the world' is sweeping and universal — not some Christians but all who are genuinely regenerate. The instrument of this victory is faith — not a heroic achievement but the ongoing trust that holds on to the risen Christ who has already overcome the world (John 16:33)."}
];

